A: I don't see a mistake in your code as it should perform the operations without throwing any exceptions. The ArrayList class should support all the operations that you implement. Your understanding of the PexObserve.ValueForViewing seems correct, it will display the values for debugging. 
However, you may want to validate the output of Pex. It automatically adds the output parameters to the PexMethod with '[PexAssumeValid]', so you don't need to validate the output again as you are already assured from Pex tests.
[TestFixture]
public class ArrayListTest
{
    [Test, PexMethod]
    public void RemoveItem_ReturnsSuccess([PexAssumeValid]ArrayList arrList, [PexAssumeValid]int item)
    {
        // arrList.Remove(item);
        bool expected = !arrList.Contains(item);
        arrList.Remove(item);
        Assert.AreEqual(expected, !arrList.Contains(item));
    }
}

This is your sample test case. It tests the Remove method of an ArrayList object. The PexMethod attribute creates new test runs with test values generated by Pex. [PexAssumeValid] attribute tells that these parameters cannot be null or out of valid range.

A: You are having an issue with NUnit not running in the debug mode.
- You may need to modify your Visual Studio Run Settings to run in Test mode and then you can select the NUnit GUI Runner before running tests. 
- Be sure to check your Test Settings by Tools->Options->NUnit.

Alternatively, you can run your tests directly from NUnit Console by right clicking in your project in Solution Explorer, choosing "Run With->NUnit Console".

Unfortunately, there is no easy solution. When tests are running, Microsoft.Pex needs extra time for instrumentation/analysis, and it interferes with the normal test execution process, causing NUnit to not run tests smoothly in Debug Mode.

Reference: https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/download/details.aspx?id=16923 

